        <div id="what-nsf-certified-for-sport-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Does NSF Certified for Sport Mean?
          </h2>

          <p>
            NSF is an independent public health and certification organization founded in 1944, best known for food equipment and drinking water standards. Its Certified for Sport program applies to dietary supplements and sports nutrition products, and it bundles several checks into one mark.
          </p>

          <p>
            First, a product must already meet NSF's contents standard, NSF/ANSI 173, which means NSF has verified that what is on the label is in the product in the stated amounts, and that the product does not contain unsafe levels of contaminants such as heavy metals, pesticides or microbes. Second, the product is screened for a panel of substances banned in sport, which NSF currently puts at around 290, including stimulants, anabolic agents, diuretics and masking agents. Third, the manufacturing facility is audited for good manufacturing practices, and certified products are subject to ongoing monitoring rather than a one-off test.
          </p>

          <p>
            That combination is why the program is recognized by major sports organizations, including the NFL, MLB, the PGA Tour and the LPGA, many of which require or strongly encourage their athletes to use only NSF Certified for Sport supplements. For a protein powder, it is about as much independent assurance as you can get on a supplement shelf.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT IT IS NOT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Certification is not a guarantee</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">No program tests every scoop, and the banned-substance panel does not cover every substance on the World Anti-Doping Agency list. NSF certification greatly reduces the risk of a contaminated product; it does not eliminate it. Anti-doping rules still hold athletes responsible for anything in their system.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Why does this matter for protein powder in particular? In the US, supplements do not need FDA approval before sale, and independent investigations have repeatedly found products with less protein than claimed, undeclared ingredients or contaminants. Our article on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers the regulatory gap, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">heavy metals guide</a> explains what contaminant testing has found.
          </p>
        </div>

