        <div id="contents-certified-vs-sport" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. NSF Certified for Sport vs NSF Contents Certified
          </h2>

          <p>
            NSF runs more than one program, and the marks are easy to confuse. NSF Contents Certified, based on the NSF/ANSI 173 standard, verifies label claims and screens for contaminants. It is a meaningful quality mark for everyday buyers. But it does not include testing for banned substances, so it offers no specific protection to an athlete who is drug tested.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF Certified for Sport builds on top of Contents Certified. A product can only carry the sport mark if it already meets the contents standard and is made in an NSF-certified GMP facility, and then it adds the banned-substance screening. The two marks look different: the contents mark is blue and reads Contents Certified, while the sport mark is blue and orange and reads Certified for Sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            A third label causes the most confusion: NSF GMP Registration. That means a manufacturing facility has been audited for good manufacturing practices. It says the factory follows good procedures; it does not mean any particular product made there has been tested. Some brands print "made in an NSF registered facility" prominently, and buyers reasonably read it as product certification. It is not.
          </p>

