        <div id="nsf-vs-informed-sport" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. NSF Certified for Sport vs Informed Sport vs Informed Choice
          </h2>

          <p>
            The other major certifier on protein powder is LGC, a UK-based testing company that runs Informed Sport and Informed Choice. Both are widely recognized internationally and both screen for banned substances using accredited lab methods. The difference between them is how often products are tested.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed Sport tests every batch of a certified product before it is released for sale, and buyers can look up individual batch numbers on its website. Informed Choice is a retail monitoring program: LGC buys certified products from shops and websites and tests samples throughout the year, at least twelve batches a year according to LGC, but not every batch. Both are respected; Informed Sport is the stricter of the two on batch coverage.
          </p>

          <p>
            How does NSF compare? NSF Certified for Sport goes further than Informed's programs in one respect, verifying the label's contents and screening for contaminants as part of the standard, and less far in another, since it does not test every batch the way Informed Sport does. In practice, sports organizations and anti-doping agencies generally treat NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport as comparable top-tier options. USADA's guidance to athletes who use supplements is to choose products certified by a third-party program that tests for banned substances; check with your own league or governing body for the programs it recognizes.
          </p>

