            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard NSF certified?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Partly. When we checked the registry on November 8, 2026, Gold Standard 100% Whey in Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream were listed as NSF Certified for Sport. Other flavors were not. If certification matters to you, buy one of those flavors and check for the NSF mark on the tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does NSF certification mean a protein powder has no heavy metals?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No product is completely free of trace heavy metals, since they occur naturally in soil and water. NSF certification means the product stays under NSF's contaminant limits. It does not rank certified products against each other, so if heavy metals are your priority, also look at published independent test results.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are NSF certified protein powders more expensive?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Often, but not always. Thorne, Klean and Momentous are premium-priced, while certified flavors of Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard and Muscle Milk Genuine are among the cheapest well-known powders per gram. Certification is now available at most price points, so it rarely forces a big trade-off.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do I need NSF certified protein if I am not an athlete?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  You do not need it, but it is a useful quality filter. Certification confirms that the protein on the label is in the tub and that contaminants are screened, which matters to anyone using a powder daily. If you are not drug tested, NSF Contents Certified or Informed Choice offer similar quality assurance.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
